questions.stem:
A loop records what it adds on each pass and then prints the loop counter after the loop has finished. What does the printer output?
public class I001 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        int i = 0;
        for (i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            log = log + "b" + i;
        }
        System.out.println(log + ":" + i);
    }
}

A loop prints its running total on every pass, adding the loop counter to that total. What does the printer output?
public class I002 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            total = total + i;
            System.out.println(total);
        }
    }
}

A loop records a t each time its guard is tested and a b for each pass, so the log shows exactly when the guard was asked. What does the printer output?
public class I003 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        int i = 0;
        while (true) {
            log = log + "t";
            if (i >= 2) {
                break;
            }
            log = log + "b";
            i++;
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A loop adds each counter value to a total, except that the value two is skipped by a continue. What does the printer output?
public class I004 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int seen = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
            if (i == 2) {
                continue;
            }
            seen = seen + i;
        }
        System.out.println(seen);
    }
}

A helper scans every pair of values in a row and returns as soon as a pair adds up to a target, packing the two positions with the row position in the tens place. What does the printer output?
public class I005 {
    static int firstHit(int[] values, int target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < values.length; j++) {
                if (values[i] + values[j] == target) {
                    return i * 10 + j;
                }
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {1, 4, 7};
        System.out.println(firstHit(values, 8));
    }
}

A loop gives each pass a local variable of its own, doubles the counter into it, and adds it to a total declared outside the loop. What does the printer output?
public class I006 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int sum = 0;
        int i = 0;
        for (i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
            int doubled = i * 2;
            sum = sum + doubled;
        }
        System.out.println(sum + ":" + i);
    }
}

A pair of nested loops prints both counters on every pass. What does the printer output?
public class I007 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
                System.out.println("" + i + j);
            }
        }
    }
}

A nested loop prints a label built from the row and the column, and a bar after each row. What does the printer output?
public class I008 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= 2; row++) {
            for (int col = 1; col <= 3; col++) {
                System.out.print(row * 10 + col);
            }
            System.out.print("|");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

A loop counts its passes in one variable while its body also advances the loop counter, and the program prints both at the end. What does the printer output?
public class I009 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int count = 0;
        int i = 0;
        for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            count++;
            i++;
        }
        System.out.println(count + ":" + i);
    }
}

A statement adds to a cell of a row using an index that advances as part of the same statement, and the program prints the row beside the index. What does the printer output?
public class I010 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {1, 2, 3};
        int index = 0;
        values[index++] += 10;
        System.out.println(values[0] + ":" + values[1] + ":" + index);
    }
}

A program prints a running figure, then lowers it by thirty on each of two passes, printing after each change. What does the printer output?
public class I011 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int running = 100;
        System.out.println(running);
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            running = running - 30;
            System.out.println(running);
        }
    }
}

A program stores one value in a fresh row and then totals the whole row. What does the printer output?
public class I012 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = new int[4];
        values[0] = 5;
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            total = total + values[i];
        }
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}

A loop adds one per pass inside a try block, adds ten in a finally block, and breaks out of the loop on the second pass. What does the printer output?
public class I013 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int log = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            try {
                if (i == 1) {
                    break;
                }
                log = log + 1;
            } finally {
                log = log + 10;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A helper returns a running total from inside a loop, and a finally block adds a hundred to that total on every pass. What does the printer output?
public class I014 {
    static int run(int[] values) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            try {
                if (i == 1) {
                    return total;
                }
                total = total + values[i];
            } finally {
                total = total + 100;
            }
        }
        return total;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(run(new int[] {5, 6, 7}));
    }
}

A try block records one, throws, and is followed by a catch block that records ten and a finally block that records a hundred. What does the printer output?
public class I015 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int log = 0;
        try {
            log = log + 1;
            throw new IllegalStateException("stop");
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            log = log + 10;
        } finally {
            log = log + 100;
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A loop inside a try block adds one at the start of every pass and ten at the end, and throws on the second pass, which a catch clause turns into a hundred. What does the printer output?
public class I016 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int log = 0;
        try {
            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
                log = log + 1;
                if (i == 1) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException("stop");
                }
                log = log + 10;
            }
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            log = log + 100;
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

Each call prints its own label as it runs, and three calls are combined in one expression. What does the printer output?
public class I017 {
    static int mark(String label, int value) {
        System.out.print(label);
        return value;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int result = mark("A", 1) + mark("B", 2) * mark("C", 3);
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}

A helper prints the value it is given and returns twice that value, and the program makes three calls. What does the printer output?
public class I018 {
    static int twice(int v) {
        System.out.print("t" + v);
        return v * 2;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = twice(1);
        int b = twice(2) + twice(3);
        System.out.println(":" + a + ":" + b);
    }
}

A factory records each object it builds, and two factory calls are chained into one expression. What does the printer output?
public class I019 {
    static StringBuilder log = new StringBuilder();
    static class Box {
        String name;
        Box(String n) { name = n; }
        String get() { return name; }
    }
    static Box make(String name) {
        log.append(name);
        return new Box(name);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String result = make("A").get() + make("B").get();
        System.out.println(log + "|" + result);
    }
}

A helper prints its label as it runs, and two calls are combined with an and. What does the printer output?
public class I020 {
    static boolean note(String label, boolean value) {
        System.out.print(label);
        return value;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean result = note("A", false) && note("B", true);
        System.out.println(":" + result);
    }
}

A second helper prints its label as it runs, and its two calls are joined with an or rather than an and. What does the printer output?
public class I021 {
    static boolean note(String label, boolean value) {
        System.out.print(label);
        return value;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean result = note("A", true) || note("B", false);
        System.out.println(":" + result);
    }
}

A program guards a division with a test on the divisor and records a flag only when the quotient is greater than one. What does the printer output?
public class I022 {
    static int divide(int a, int b) {
        return a / b;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int b = 0;
        int result = 0;
        if (b != 0 && divide(10, b) > 1) {
            result = 1;
        }
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}

A helper prints a marker as it runs, and a conditional expression picks between two calls to it. What does the printer output?
public class I023 {
    static int twice(int v) {
        System.out.print("t");
        return v * 2;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int flag = 0;
        int result = flag == 0 ? twice(5) : twice(9);
        System.out.println(":" + result + ":" + flag);
    }
}

A helper advances a counter and returns the position it has just left, and a row is read using that helper as its index. What does the printer output?
public class I024 {
    static int index = 0;
    static int[] data = {4, 7, 9};
    static int next() {
        index = index + 1;
        return index - 1;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int value = data[next()];
        System.out.println(value + ":" + index);
    }
}

A program records that it has entered a try block, then writes past the end of a short row, and records again in the catch block. Which line correctly describes what the printer outputs?
public class I025 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        try {
            log = log + "t";
            int[] values = new int[1];
            values[2] = 5;
        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            log = log + "c";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A program catches an exception with two catch clauses, one for the exact type and one for a supertype of it. Which line correctly describes what the printer outputs?
public class I026 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        try {
            log = log + "t";
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("bad");
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            log = log + "a";
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            log = log + "r";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

Two resources are declared in a try with resources block, each recording when it is opened and when it is closed, and the body records itself. What does the printer output?
public class I027 {
    static String log = "";
    static class Res implements AutoCloseable {
        String name;
        Res(String n) { name = n; log = log + "open" + n; }
        public void close() { log = log + "close" + name; }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Res first = new Res("A"); Res second = new Res("B")) {
            log = log + "body";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A loop opens a fresh resource on each of two passes, records its own work, and the closing method records the pass it belongs to. What does the printer output?
public class I028 {
    static String log = "";
    static class Res implements AutoCloseable {
        int pass;
        Res(int p) { pass = p; }
        public void close() { log = log + "c" + pass; }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
            try (Res r = new Res(i)) {
                log = log + "b" + i;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A loop runs a switch on its counter and records the counter after the switch, with one counter value skipped by a continue written inside the switch. What does the printer output?
public class I029 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            switch (i) {
                case 1:
                    continue;
                default:
                    log = log + i;
            }
            log = log + ".";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A loop runs a switch on its counter, marks the first counter value with a z, and records a bar after each pass. What does the printer output?
public class I030 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            switch (i) {
                case 0:
                    log = log + "z";
                    break;
                default:
                    log = log + i;
            }
            log = log + "|";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A loop runs a switch on its counter in which two counter values share a body, one case falls through into the default, and the letters are collected. What does the printer output?
public class I031 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            switch (i) {
                case 0:
                case 1:
                    log = log + "a";
                    break;
                case 2:
                    log = log + "b";
                default:
                    log = log + "c";
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A labelled outer loop holds an inner loop, and one combination of the two counters restarts the outer loop. What does the printer output?
public class I032 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        outer:
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
                if (i + j == 1) {
                    continue outer;
                }
                log = log + i + "" + j + " ";
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log.trim());
    }
}

A labelled outer loop holds an inner loop, and the one pair of counters that matches ends the whole nested structure at once. What does the printer output?
public class I033 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        outer:
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
                if (i * j == 2) {
                    break outer;
                }
                log = log + i + j + " ";
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log.trim());
    }
}

A loop records its counter on every pass and throws on the third pass, which a catch clause turns into an exclamation mark. What does the printer output?
public class I034 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        try {
            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                log = log + i;
                if (i == 2) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException("stop");
                }
            }
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            log = log + "!";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A loop records its counter on every pass, except that the second pass throws and is caught inside the loop, which records a c instead. What does the printer output?
public class I035 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            try {
                if (i == 1) {
                    throw new IllegalStateException("x");
                }
                log = log + i;
            } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
                log = log + "c";
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A program stores one value at the first index, advances the index, and then stores the value of the cell before it plus one. What does the printer output?
public class I036 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = new int[3];
        int i = 0;
        values[i] = i + 1;
        i++;
        values[i] = values[i - 1] + 1;
        System.out.println(values[0] + ":" + values[1] + ":" + i);
    }
}

One loop fills a row with one, two, and three, and a second loop walks back over the same row multiplying each cell by ten. What does the printer output?
public class I037 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = new int[3];
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            values[i] = i + 1;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            values[i] = values[i] * 10;
        }
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(values));
    }
}

A loop counts how many neighbouring cells are equal and then adds ten to the cell it has just looked at, so the next pass sees the changed value. What does the printer output?
public class I038 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {2, 2, 3};
        int same = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {
            if (values[i] == values[i - 1]) {
                same++;
            }
            values[i] = values[i] + 10;
        }
        System.out.println(same + ":" + values[0] + ":" + values[1] + ":" + values[2]);
    }
}

Nested enhanced for loops add up every cell of a grid whose rows have different lengths and count the cells they visit. What does the printer output?
public class I039 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] grid = {{1, 2}, {3, 4, 5}};
        int total = 0;
        int cells = 0;
        for (int[] row : grid) {
            for (int cell : row) {
                total = total + cell;
                cells++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(total + ":" + cells);
    }
}

An enhanced for loop adds up a row while the body keeps its own counter of how many elements it has seen. What does the printer output?
public class I040 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {4, 8, 15};
        int seen = 0;
        int index = 0;
        for (int v : values) {
            seen = seen + v;
            index++;
        }
        System.out.println(seen + ":" + index);
    }
}

A loop adds its counter to a running total and also lowers the bound by one on every pass, so the test sees a bound that keeps shrinking. What does the printer output?
public class I041 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int limit = 6;
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < limit; i++) {
            total = total + i;
            limit = limit - 1;
        }
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}

A loop takes a fresh local on every pass, fills it from the running total, and then rebuilds the total from that local and the counter. What does the printer output?
public class I042 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
            int pass = total;
            total = pass + i;
        }
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}

A loop tests its guard by calling a method that records how many times it has been called and returns a fixed limit. What does the printer output?
public class I043 {
    static int ticks = 0;
    static int guard() {
        ticks = ticks + 1;
        return 2;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < guard(); i++) {
            System.out.print(i);
        }
        System.out.println(":" + ticks);
    }
}

One loop adds its counter three times and a second loop doubles the running total twice. What does the printer output?
public class I044 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            total = total + i;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            total = total * 2;
        }
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}

One loop adds a row of values into a running total, and a second loop adds that total once for every element of the same row. What does the printer output?
public class I045 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {3, 1, 4};
        int index = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            index = index + values[i];
        }
        int other = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            other = other + index;
        }
        System.out.println(index + ":" + other);
    }
}

A loop stores the carried value plus the counter in each cell and then carries the value it has just stored into the next pass. What does the printer output?
public class I046 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = new int[4];
        int previous = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            values[i] = previous + i;
            previous = values[i];
        }
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(values) + ":" + previous);
    }
}

An outer loop holds an inner loop that adds a pair of counters, and a statement after the inner loop adds the outer counter once more on every outer pass. What does the printer output?
public class I047 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 0;
        int i = 0;
        for (i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            int j = 0;
            for (j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
                total = total + i * 10 + j;
            }
            total = total + i;
        }
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}

A helper counts the passes after the first, except that it stops on the third pass, so it should print 1. The loop below prints 0 because its outer test starts too late. Which replacement for the outer test makes it print 1?
public class I048 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int hits = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            if (i > 1) {
                if (i == 2) {
                    break;
                }
                hits++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(hits);
    }
}

A loop records its counter on every pass and copies the next cell into the current one, which fails on the last pass and is caught inside the loop. What does the printer output?
public class I049 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        int[] values = {1, 2, 3};
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            try {
                log = log + i;
                values[i] = values[i + 1];
            } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
                log = log + "c";
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A try block holds a second try block that throws, and each level has its own catch clause and its own finally clause that each record a letter. What does the printer output?
public class I050 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        try {
            log = log + "o";
            try {
                log = log + "i";
                throw new IllegalStateException("x");
            } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
                log = log + "ic";
            } finally {
                log = log + "if";
            }
        } finally {
            log = log + "of";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A try block records a t and completes normally, with a catch clause and a finally clause beside it that would each record a letter. What does the printer output?
public class I051 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        try {
            log = log + "t";
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            log = log + "c";
        } finally {
            log = log + "f";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

An outer loop holds an inner loop, and one combination of the two counters throws, which a catch clause around the inner loop turns into a mark. What does the printer output?
public class I052 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            try {
                for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
                    log = log + i + "" + j + " ";
                    if (i == 1 && j == 1) {
                        throw new IllegalStateException("x");
                    }
                }
            } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
                log = log + "!";
            }
        }
        System.out.println(log.trim());
    }
}

A helper records every call it receives and returns a fixed value, and four of its calls are combined in one expression. What does the printer output?
public class I053 {
    static int calls = 0;
    static int step(int v) {
        calls = calls + 1;
        return v;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int result = step(1) + step(2) * step(3) - step(4);
        System.out.println(result + ":" + calls);
    }
}

A nested pair of try blocks throws from the inner one, and each level has a finally clause and an outer catch clause that each record a letter. What does the printer output?
public class I054 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String log = "";
        try {
            try {
                log = log + "a";
                throw new IllegalStateException("x");
            } finally {
                log = log + "b";
            }
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            log = log + "c";
        } finally {
            log = log + "d";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

A helper returns the position of the first negative value, and a finally clause adds a hundred to every cell it has passed, and the caller prints the position beside one of those cells. What does the printer output?
public class I055 {
    static int scan(int[] values) {
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            try {
                if (values[i] < 0) {
                    return i;
                }
            } finally {
                values[i] = values[i] + 100;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {3, -4, 5};
        System.out.println(scan(values) + ":" + values[1]);
    }
}

Two resources are declared in a try with resources block whose body records itself and then throws, and a catch clause records the handling. What does the printer output?
public class I056 {
    static String log = "";
    static class Res implements AutoCloseable {
        String name;
        Res(String n) { name = n; log = log + "open" + n; }
        public void close() { log = log + "close" + name; }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Res a = new Res("A"); Res b = new Res("B")) {
            log = log + "body";
            throw new IllegalStateException("x");
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            log = log + "catch";
        }
        System.out.println(log);
    }
}

